+### Neuralangelo
+A framework for high-fidelity 3D surface reconstruction from RGB images using neural volume rendering, combining multi-resolution 3D hash grids with neural surface rendering without auxiliary data [S3]. Uses numerical gradients to compute higher-order derivatives (e.g., surface normals) as a smoothing operation to stabilize optimization [S3]. Uses coarse-to-fine optimization on hash grids controlling different levels of details [S3]. Samples 3D locations along camera view directions [S3]. Encodes the sampled positions using a multi-resolution hash encoding [S3]. Feeds encoded features into an SDF MLP and a color MLP [S3]. Composites images using SDF-based volume rendering [S3]. Unlike concurrent work [43] which uses a displacement network to correct shape, Neuralangelo uses hierarchical hash grids and controls detail levels based on analysis of higher-order derivatives [S3].
+
+### Volumetric Ambient Occlusion prediction
+A method that predicts per-voxel ambient occlusion volumes using 3D convolutional encoder-decoders with implicit transfer function representation [S4, unofficial]. Resolution: 128×128×128 [S4, unofficial]. Limited to coarse resolution due to 3D CNN computational cost [S4, unofficial]. Performance: Computed volumetric AO in 411ms (SSIM 0.875) [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Neural volume rendering emerged from the field of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF), introduced by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to address the limitations of explicit 3D representations (like meshes and voxels) in novel view synthesis, particularly for complex, unstructured scenes. It builds on the concept of implicit neural representations, where a scene is defined by a continuous function parameterized by a neural network [S1, unofficial]. The key innovation was the integration of differentiable volume rendering, allowing the network to be trained directly from 2D images using gradient descent [S1, unofficial]. Subsequent developments, such as DVGO and Instant NGP, have focused on improving training and inference speed by using voxel grids and hash encodings instead of pure MLPs [S1, unofficial].
+Neural volume rendering originated with the introduction of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) by Mildenhall et al. in 2020 [S1, S14]. The method leverages the differentiability of the volume rendering integral to optimize a neural network's weights such that the rendered images match ground truth photographs [S1, unofficial]. This allows for the synthesis of novel views of complex 3D scenes from a sparse set of 2D images. The technique has since evolved into various forms, including hash-grid based methods like Instant NGP [S1, unofficial], voxel-based methods like DVGO [S1, unofficial], and surface-based methods like Neuralangelo [S3]. It is widely used in computer vision for 3D reconstruction, augmented reality, and novel view synthesis [S1, S14].
 
